1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
This manhole cover is round for a reason.

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:04,800
A thin square cover can slip through its

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,640
own hole if you tilt and turn it.

4
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:08,400
A round one never can.

5
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:11,040
Here's the geometry, and a strange shape

6
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:12,320
that works as well.

7
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:14,560
Let's prove it with a little geometry.

8
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:17,440
First, how does a cover stay up at all?

9
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:19,440
Around the top of the hole there's a small

10
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:20,800
ledge, called a lip.

11
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,400
The cover is slightly bigger than the

12
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:24,320
opening, so it rests on that lip.

13
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,640
To fall in, the cover would have to tip up

14
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,720
on its edge and find a gap in the opening

15
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:30,880
that's wider than the cover itself.

16
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,760
Now try a square cover with side s.

17
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:35,920
The longest line across a square is its

18
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:38,480
diagonal. By Pythagoras, the diagonal is

19
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:40,240
the square root of s squared plus s

20
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:42,480
squared. That's s times the square root of

21
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:44,560
two, about one point four one times the

22
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:47,200
side. So tip the square cover up on its

23
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,960
edge and turn it to line up with the

24
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:50,240
opening's diagonal.

25
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:52,000
If it's thin enough, it slips right

26
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:52,720
through.

27
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:54,240
Let's put in real numbers.

28
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:56,400
Say a square cover is sixty centimeters

29
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,320
across, and the lip is two centimeters

30
00:00:58,320 --> 00:00:59,120
wide all around.

31
00:00:59,490 --> 00:01:01,650
The opening is then fifty six centimeters

32
00:01:01,650 --> 00:01:04,130
across. Its diagonal is fifty six times

33
00:01:04,130 --> 00:01:06,210
one point four one, about seventy nine

34
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:08,210
centimeters. That's plenty of room for a

35
00:01:08,210 --> 00:01:10,610
sixty centimeter cover tipped on its edge.

36
00:01:10,610 --> 00:01:11,970
Now make it round.

37
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:13,970
The widest line across the opening is just

38
00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:16,370
fifty six centimeters, while the cover is

39
00:01:16,370 --> 00:01:18,130
sixty in every direction.

40
00:01:18,130 --> 00:01:19,570
It can't fit.

41
00:01:19,570 --> 00:01:21,250
It's not just squares.

42
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:23,490
A rectangular cover can also slip through

43
00:01:23,490 --> 00:01:25,410
a suitable opening when it's tilted and

44
00:01:25,410 --> 00:01:27,810
turned. An equilateral triangle has a

45
00:01:27,810 --> 00:01:28,770
similar weakness.

46
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:31,010
Its height is only about eighty seven

47
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:33,650
percent of its side, so it has a narrow

48
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:35,730
direction that can let it through.

49
00:01:35,730 --> 00:01:38,210
The key idea is that most shapes have

50
00:01:38,210 --> 00:01:40,530
different widths in different directions.

51
00:01:40,530 --> 00:01:42,930
Whether a real cover actually passes also

52
00:01:42,930 --> 00:01:44,690
depends on its thickness and the shape of

53
00:01:44,690 --> 00:01:45,810
the opening.

54
00:01:45,810 --> 00:01:47,410
A circle is different.

55
00:01:47,410 --> 00:01:49,410
Measure it in any direction, and you get

56
00:01:49,410 --> 00:01:51,410
the same number, its diameter.

57
00:01:51,410 --> 00:01:53,410
This is called constant width.

58
00:01:53,570 --> 00:01:56,050
Picture two parallel plates squeezing the

59
00:01:56,050 --> 00:01:58,140
cover. However you turn a circle, the

60
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:00,380
plates stay the same distance apart.

61
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:03,020
So a rigid round cover's narrowest width

62
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:05,420
is its full diameter, bigger than any line

63
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:07,100
across the smaller round opening.

64
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:08,140
There's no way in.

65
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:10,380
The circle isn't the only shape of

66
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:11,580
constant width.

67
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:14,700
Draw an equilateral triangle with side w.

68
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:17,100
Put a compass point on one corner and draw

69
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:18,940
an arc joining the other two.

70
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:20,460
Repeat from each corner.

71
00:02:20,460 --> 00:02:22,300
You get a curved triangle called a

72
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:24,380
Reuleaux triangle, named after the German

73
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:26,140
engineer Franz Reuleaux.

74
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:28,220
Squeeze it between parallel plates, and

75
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:31,100
the gap is w in every direction.

76
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:33,260
And it has less area.

77
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:36,460
A circle of width w has an area of pi over

78
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:38,220
four, times w squared.

79
00:02:38,220 --> 00:02:40,380
A Reuleaux triangle of the same width has

80
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:43,020
an area of pi minus root three, over two,

81
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:44,620
times w squared.

82
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:47,180
That's about ten point three percent less.

83
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:49,260
For flat plates of equal thickness, that

84
00:02:49,260 --> 00:02:51,100
would mean less metal, although real

85
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:53,340
covers also need strength and a supporting

86
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:55,740
frame. Of all shapes with the same

87
00:02:55,740 --> 00:02:58,060
constant width, the Reuleaux triangle has

88
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:00,220
the smallest area, and the circle the

89
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:02,790
largest. Yet every one of them has the

90
00:03:02,790 --> 00:03:05,830
same perimeter, pi times w.

91
00:03:05,830 --> 00:03:08,630
So do we use these shapes in real life?

92
00:03:08,630 --> 00:03:11,270
In San Francisco, some small water valve

93
00:03:11,270 --> 00:03:12,550
covers in the street are shaped like

94
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:14,390
Reuleaux triangles, which helps set

95
00:03:14,390 --> 00:03:15,910
certain water lines apart.

96
00:03:15,910 --> 00:03:18,070
Those are small valve covers, not full

97
00:03:18,150 --> 00:03:19,750
size manhole covers.

98
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:21,900
And in Britain, the twenty pence and fifty

99
00:03:21,910 --> 00:03:24,390
pence coins are seven sided curves of

100
00:03:24,390 --> 00:03:25,430
constant width.

101
00:03:25,430 --> 00:03:27,140
That lets them roll and be measured

102
00:03:27,150 --> 00:03:28,870
consistently in vending machines, however

103
00:03:28,870 --> 00:03:30,070
they're turned.

104
00:03:30,070 --> 00:03:32,390
Constant width has one more trick.

105
00:03:32,390 --> 00:03:34,470
Lay a flat board on rollers shaped like

106
00:03:34,470 --> 00:03:36,150
Reuleaux triangles, and push.

107
00:03:36,150 --> 00:03:38,470
The board glides along perfectly level,

108
00:03:38,470 --> 00:03:40,230
because the floor and the board always

109
00:03:40,230 --> 00:03:41,750
stay w apart.

110
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:44,150
The rollers' centers bob up and down, but

111
00:03:44,150 --> 00:03:45,030
the board doesn't.

112
00:03:45,190 --> 00:03:47,110
A fixed axle is different.

113
00:03:47,110 --> 00:03:49,270
Put a Reuleaux wheel on an ordinary axle

114
00:03:49,270 --> 00:03:50,950
through its center, and the distance from

115
00:03:50,950 --> 00:03:53,270
axle to ground keeps changing, so the ride

116
00:03:53,270 --> 00:03:54,470
is bumpy.

117
00:03:54,470 --> 00:03:56,950
So why aren't all manhole covers Reuleaux

118
00:03:56,950 --> 00:03:59,750
triangles? Because not falling in is only

119
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:01,430
one design consideration.

120
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:03,980
A round cover can go back on in any

121
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:06,300
orientation, with no corners to line up.

122
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:08,540
A heavy round cover can be rolled instead

123
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:11,020
of carried. And many access shafts are

124
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:13,100
round, so a round cover is a practical

125
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:15,420
match. Other shapes can work too, with

126
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:18,380
well designed frames, hinges or locks.

127
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:20,620
But the circle combines constant width

128
00:04:20,620 --> 00:04:23,420
with several practical advantages.

129
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:25,260
So here's the principle.

130
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:27,740
A rigid round cover can't pass through a

131
00:04:27,740 --> 00:04:29,420
smaller round opening, because its

132
00:04:29,420 --> 00:04:31,420
diameter is bigger than the opening's,

133
00:04:31,420 --> 00:04:32,700
however you turn it.

134
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:34,940
A square doesn't get that protection.

135
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:37,500
Its diagonal is about one point four one

136
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:39,740
times its side, which can open a route

137
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:41,980
through. And the Reuleaux triangle shows

138
00:04:41,980 --> 00:04:43,820
that constant width isn't just for

139
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:46,220
circles. That's the clever idea behind the

140
00:04:46,220 --> 00:04:47,820
round cover on your street.

141
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:49,500
If you liked this video, please give it a

142
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:51,180
like and subscribe for more everyday

143
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:52,860
engineering. Thanks for watching!
